# Normalize line endings so measurements are reproducible across platforms.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Hardhat's coverage instrumentation maps executed bytecode
# back to source by BYTE OFFSET, so a CRLF checkout and an LF checkout do not
# produce the same coverage numbers from identical code. Measured on this repo:
#
#   CRLF checkout (Windows default)   94.37% line coverage
#   LF checkout   (Linux, CI)         89.22% line coverage
#
# Same source, same solc, same tests. A five-point difference with no code change.
#
# That gap is how a coverage floor calibrated on one developer's machine becomes
# a red build on CI — which is exactly what happened: the floor was set to 93%
# from a local reading of 94.37%, and CI failed on its first real run at 89.22%.
#
# Forcing LF for Solidity makes the two agree, so the number means the same thing
# everywhere and a floor can be trusted.
#
# Nothing here is about how the files are *edited* — editors may still write CRLF.
# Git normalizes on commit and checkout.
#
# Note this only re-normalizes going forward. Existing files with CRLF in the
# working tree stay CRLF until re-checked out:
#
#   git add --renormalize . && git checkout -- .
#
# Solidity is what coverage measures, so it is what must be pinned. The rest are
# listed for consistency and to stop the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings
# on every commit.

*.sol text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF or cmd.exe will not run them.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary — never normalized.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
